1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,944
In Episode 3, we mapped Aalayam's competitors and used perceptual maps to find where the brand could stand apart.

2
00:00:06,864 --> 00:00:15,952
But knowing the competition is only half the story. We also need to understand the customers: what they like, what frustrates them, and what they're still looking for.

3
00:00:15,872 --> 00:00:22,605
So in Episode 4, we move from competitive positioning to customer sentiment, and to the size of the opportunity.

4
00:00:23,185 --> 00:00:31,767
Customer sentiment analysis means reading what people say about products, in reviews, comments and conversations, and working out how they feel.

5
00:00:31,686 --> 00:00:41,115
In clothing, people rarely talk only about the design. They talk about comfort, fabric, fit, sizing, durability, price, and whether it looks like the pictures.

6
00:00:41,035 --> 00:00:47,190
Here are three summaries of public reviews on Myntra. They're other brands' products, shown only as examples.

7
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:56,387
Positive: an Anouk cotton kurta set rated four point four, where buyers describe soft, breathable fabric, a comfortable fit and stitching that holds up.

8
00:00:56,307 --> 00:01:03,455
Negative: another Anouk set rated three point seven, with complaints about sheer fabric, small trousers and weak stitching.

9
00:01:03,375 --> 00:01:08,100
And mixed: a set buyers find soft and breathable, but whose sizing ran tight.

10
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:17,952
We gathered feedback from Myntra, Amazon India and AJIO reviews, Instagram comments, and YouTube haul and review videos. That's our secondary research.

11
00:01:17,872 --> 00:01:25,272
Our primary research came from customer interviews. Customer surveys are the next step, to test what we found at a larger scale.

12
00:01:25,852 --> 00:01:28,379
Here's how we analysed it, in five steps.

13
00:01:28,299 --> 00:01:40,412
First, collect reviews and feedback. Second, clean the data: remove duplicates, spam and unusable comments. Some reviews even contained unfilled template text, a sign they may not be genuine.

14
00:01:40,332 --> 00:01:51,235
Third, classify each one as positive, negative, neutral or mixed. Fourth, tag the themes: comfort, fabric, fit and sizing, transparency, colour, price, delivery and returns.

15
00:01:51,155 --> 00:01:57,768
And fifth, turn the patterns into insights: what customers value, what they complain about, and what they wish existed.

16
00:01:58,348 --> 00:02:01,375
In the reviews we read, three themes kept coming back.

17
00:02:01,295 --> 00:02:11,306
Comfort and soft, breathable fabric earned the most praise. Fit and sizing caused the most frustration. And colour or sheerness that didn't match the photos created doubt.

18
00:02:11,226 --> 00:02:23,067
Each pain point can become an opportunity: clearer size guidance for fit, better fabric choices for quality, comfort-led design, honest value, and true-to-life photos with transparent descriptions.

19
00:02:22,987 --> 00:02:29,599
These are potential opportunities, not confirmed Aalayam insights. They need testing with Aalayam's own customers.

20
00:02:30,179 --> 00:02:35,428
Sentiment tells us what people want. Market sizing tells us how big that opportunity is.

21
00:02:35,348 --> 00:02:43,618
We use three layers: TAM, the total addressable market; SAM, the part Aalayam can serve; and SOM, the part it could realistically win.

22
00:02:44,198 --> 00:02:58,721
For the total market, we used Technopak's industry report from August twenty twenty-five. Indian women's apparel was worth about two point three trillion rupees in financial year twenty twenty-four, heading for about four trillion by twenty twenty-nine.

23
00:02:58,641 --> 00:03:09,287
But Aalayam doesn't compete in all of it. We kept only the categories it sells: sarees, at five hundred and sixty-four billion rupees, and sets, at four hundred and fifteen billion.

24
00:03:09,207 --> 00:03:13,019
That gives a TAM of roughly nine hundred and eighty billion rupees.

25
00:03:13,599 --> 00:03:16,730
Next, we narrowed it to what Aalayam can actually serve.

26
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:23,558
Online sales were about twenty percent of women's apparel, so that leaves around one hundred and ninety-six billion rupees.

27
00:03:23,478 --> 00:03:29,878
Aalayam sits in the mid-price segment, about twenty-seven percent of the market, which leaves around fifty-three billion.

28
00:03:29,798 --> 00:03:39,598
And women aged twenty to forty are roughly forty-four percent of adult women. That gives a SAM of about twenty-three billion rupees, around two thousand three hundred crore.

29
00:03:40,178 --> 00:03:43,754
SOM is built from the bottom up, not by picking a percentage.

30
00:03:43,674 --> 00:03:50,991
Revenue equals the customers we can reach, times the share who buy, times how often they buy, times the average order value.

31
00:03:50,911 --> 00:04:03,540
As a planning example: if Aalayam reaches two and a half million women in year three, six percent visit the store, and one and a half percent of them buy, that's about two thousand two hundred new customers, plus returning ones.

32
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:11,875
At about one point three orders a year and fifteen hundred rupees per order, year-three revenue comes to roughly fifty lakh rupees, before returns.

33
00:04:12,455 --> 00:04:27,243
That's about two hundredths of one percent of SAM. Simply taking one percent of SAM would suggest twenty-three crore, far more than this plan could support. So the bottom-up number is the realistic one, and every input can be replaced with Aalayam's real data.

34
00:04:27,823 --> 00:04:38,961
Together, these tell Aalayam where to focus: women aged twenty to forty, comfortable everyday sets and sarees, better fit and fabric information, and growth plans built on real numbers.

35
00:04:38,881 --> 00:04:50,026
Through our competitive analysis, we understood the market. Through sentiment analysis, we began to understand the customer. And through TAM, SAM and SOM, we estimated the business opportunity.

36
00:04:49,946 --> 00:04:55,314
Together, these insights help us move from research to informed product and business decisions.
